{"title":"Cascade Furniture","sub":"Late deliveries at a furniture plant: fix one machine or two?","type":"Cost & operations","industry":"Furniture manufacturing","geo":"Denmark","diff":1,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Bottleneck shifts · sequencing investments","prompt":"Cascade makes flat-pack modular shelving in Denmark and sells it across Europe. Customers want about 105,000 units a year, but shipments run late because work piles up in the lacquer finishing area. The COO wants to add a second lacquer booth for $450k. She asks whether that solves the problem.","clarify":[{"q":"Process?","a":"Three steps: cutting panels, assembly, lacquer finishing."},{"q":"Hours?","a":"One shift: about 1,820 hours a year."},{"q":"Quality?","a":"95% of units pass inspection."},{"q":"Economics?","a":"Each extra unit sold earns $24 of contribution."}],"structure":{"root":"How should Cascade raise output to meet demand?","branches":[{"head":"Capacity by step","items":["Units per hour","Bottleneck today","Bottleneck after each change"]},{"head":"Output","items":["Good units vs demand"]},{"head":"Investment","items":["Cost of each option","Payback"]}]},"hypothesis":"Finishing is where work piles up, so it's probably the bottleneck. But speeding up finishing may just move the bottleneck to the next-slowest step.","questions":[{"q":"How many good units can Cascade ship today?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"394.408156pt\" height=\"163.444pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 394.408156 163.444\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M -0 163.444 L 394.408156 163.444 L 394.408156 0 L -0 0 L -0 163.444 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 41.248156 138.336 L 387.208156 138.336 L 387.208156 10.824 L 41.248156 10.824 L 41.248156 138.336